window.SHEETS.acct3={"lang":"en","file":"study-sheet-financial-analysis","tag":"Study sheet · Financial analysis","title":"Financial statement<br>analysis","sub":"Every ratio of the course with its formula and benchmark, one worked company, the direction questions, the traps and the 13-minute exam tactics.","source":"Financial Analysis Exam Lab · cheat sheet and exam tactics","intro":"Financial statement analysis turns the numbers of the statements into <b>ratios</b> that answer four questions: can the firm pay its bills (<b>liquidity</b>), carry its debt (<b>long-term debt</b>), earn from its sales and assets (<b>profitability</b>), and how does the market value it (<b>market ratios</b>)?","sections":[{"title":"Start here (if you have read nothing)","blocks":[{"t":"p","x":"Financial statement analysis means taking the numbers of a company's statements and turning them into <b>ratios</b> that answer four simple questions. Your exam has one question on each theme (plus the overview)."},{"t":"table","head":["Theme","The question it answers","Who asks it"],"rows":[["Liquidity","Can the firm pay its bills in the next 12 months?","Suppliers, banks (short term)"],["Long-term debt-paying ability","Can it carry its debt and pay the interest over many years?","Lenders, bondholders"],["Profitability","How much does it earn from its sales and its assets?","Owners, managers"],["Market analysis","How does the stock market value the shares?","Investors"]]},{"t":"h3","x":"The two statements you need"},{"t":"key","title":"Rule","items":["<b>Balance sheet</b> (one date): Assets = Liabilities + Equity.<br>Assets: current (cash, marketable securities, receivables A/R, inventory) then fixed and intangible assets. Liabilities: current (due within a year: payables A/P, short-term debt) then long-term debt."]},{"t":"key","title":"Rule","items":["<b>Income statement</b> (one year), from top to bottom:<br>Net sales − COGS = <b>Gross profit</b> − operating expenses = <b>EBIT</b> (operating profit) − interest = <b>EBT</b> − tax = <b>Net income (NI)</b>.<br>EBITDA = EBIT + depreciation &amp; amortisation."]},{"t":"h3","x":"Memory trick"},{"t":"ul","items":["Balance-sheet items (stocks) divide <b>sales or COGS</b> to give a turnover. Days = 365 / turnover.","Liquidity = <b>current</b> items only. Solvency = <b>total</b> liabilities. Profitability = EBIT or NI over sales, assets or equity. Market = per-share numbers."]}]},{"title":"Worked example: one company, every ratio","blocks":[{"t":"p","x":"Use this single company to see where each number comes from. All figures in €."},{"t":"table","head":["Income statement","","Balance sheet",""],"rows":[["Net sales","1,000","Cash / marketable securities","50 / 30"],["COGS","600","Receivables / Inventory","120 / 200"],["Gross profit","400","<b>Current assets</b>","400"],["Operating expenses (incl. D&amp;A 50)","280","Fixed assets / Intangibles","500 / 100"],["<b>EBIT</b> (EBITDA = 170)","120","<b>Total assets</b>","1,000"],["Interest","20","Current liabilities","250"],["EBT","100","Long-term debt","350"],["Tax","25","<b>Total liabilities</b>","600"],["<b>Net income</b>","75","<b>Equity</b>","400"]]},{"t":"table","head":["Ratio","Calculation","Result"],"rows":[["Working capital","400 − 250","150"],["Current ratio","400 / 250","1.60"],["Quick ratio","(400 − 200) / 250","0.80"],["Cash ratio","(50 + 30) / 250","0.32"],["A/R days","365 / (1,000 / 120)","43.8"],["Inventory days","365 / (600 / 200)","121.7"],["Debt ratio","600 / 1,000","60%"],["Debt / Equity","600 / 400","1.50"],["Debt to tangible net worth","600 / (400 − 100)","2.00"],["Times interest earned","120 / 20","6.0"],["Financial debt / EBITDA (debt = 350)","350 / 170","2.1 years"],["Gross / operating / net margin","400/1,000 · 120/1,000 · 75/1,000","40% · 12% · 7.5%"],["Asset turnover","1,000 / 1,000","1.00"],["ROA","120 / 1,000 (= 12% × 1.0)","12%"],["ROE","75 / 400","18.75%"],["Leverage effect","(100/120) × (1,000/400)","2.08 (favourable)"],["DuPont check","1.0 × 12% × 0.833 × 2.5 × 0.75","18.75%"],["EPS (50 shares)","75 / 50","€1.50"],["Diluted EPS (+5 dilutive)","75 / 55","€1.36"],["P/E (price €22.5)","22.5 / 1.36","16.5"],["Payout (DPS €0.60)","0.60 / 1.36","44%"],["Market-to-book","(22.5 × 50) / 400","2.81"]]}]},{"title":"If X happens, then Y (direction questions)","blocks":[{"t":"table","head":["Event","Effect"],"rows":[["Pay a short-term debt with cash","WC same; current ratio up if &gt; 1, down if &lt; 1; quick ratio moves the same way"],["Buy inventory on credit","WC same; current ratio moves toward 1; quick ratio falls"],["Buy inventory with cash","Current ratio same; quick and cash ratios fall"],["Collect receivables","Current and quick ratios same; A/R days fall"],["Pay suppliers more slowly","A/P days up, cash cycle shorter, liquidity improves"],["Sell more with the same assets","Asset turnover and ROA up"],["Raise debt to buy back equity","Debt/equity up; ROE up if ROA &gt; interest rate, else down"],["Interest expense rises, EBIT constant","TIE falls"],["Issue dilutive securities","Diluted EPS falls, P/E on diluted EPS rises"],["Net income &gt; 0 but cash falls","Profit tied up in receivables/inventory: profit ≠ liquidity"]]}]},{"title":"Traps the exam likes","blocks":[{"t":"ul","items":["Read \"average\" or \"year-end\": average = (opening + closing)/2.","Days always = 365 / turnover. Do not divide by 360.","A/P turnover uses purchases if given, otherwise COGS.","ROA in this course = EBIT / total assets (not net income).","Debt/equity uses total liabilities, not only bank debt.","No % change if the base is 0 or the sign flips. Next year 0 = −100%.","\"Before taxes\" in ROE formula: ROA + (ROA − i) × L/E.","Negative working capital is not automatically bad: check A/R days vs A/P days.","A very high current ratio is not automatically good: idle assets.","Diluted figures: denominator includes dilutive securities, so EPS is lower."]}]},{"title":"Glossary","blocks":[{"t":"dl","items":[["<b>A/R · A/P</b>","Accounts receivable (customers owe us) · accounts payable (we owe suppliers)"],["<b>CA · CL</b>","Current assets · current liabilities (due within one year)"],["<b>COGS</b>","Cost of goods sold"],["<b>EBIT · EBT · EBITDA</b>","Earnings before interest and taxes · before taxes · before interest, taxes, depreciation and amortisation"],["<b>Marketable securities</b>","Short-term investments easily sold for cash"],["<b>Intangibles</b>","Goodwill, patents, brands"],["<b>Dilutive securities</b>","Convertible bonds, options, convertible preferred shares that could add shares"],["<b>Leverage</b>","Using borrowed money to finance assets"]]}]},{"title":"Overview","blocks":[{"t":"ul","items":["<b>Vertical (common-size):</b> each line as % of net sales (income statement) or total assets (balance sheet), same year.","<b>Horizontal:</b> same line across years. Next year = 0 → −100%. Sign flips or base year = 0 → no % (say it in words).","<b>Comparisons:</b> time series, cross-section/industry, standards (rules of thumb).","<b>Limitations:</b> formulas differ, elective accounting, fiscal year-ends, limited disclosure, earnings management, historical cost.","<b>Average balances:</b> flows (sales, COGS) over the year vs balances at one date. Seasonality still distorts."]}]},{"title":"Liquidity","blocks":[{"t":"table","head":["Measure","Formula","Benchmark"],"rows":[["Working capital","CA − CL",""],["Current ratio","CA / CL","1.5 – 2"],["Quick ratio","(CA − Inventory) / CL","≥ 1"],["Cash ratio","(Cash + marketable securities) / CL","~0.3"],["A/R turnover · days","Net sales / A/R · 365 / turnover",""],["Inventory turnover · days","COGS / Inventory · 365 / turnover",""],["A/P turnover · days","Purchases (or COGS) / A/P · 365 / turnover",""],["Cash cycle","A/R days + Inventory days − A/P days","lower is better"]]},{"t":"key","title":"Rule","items":["\"With average\" means (opening + closing)/2. If an allowance for doubtful accounts is given, days' sales in receivables uses <b>gross</b> receivables."]},{"t":"h3","x":"Reasoning rules"},{"t":"ul","items":["Negative working capital is fine if A/R days &lt; A/P days (cash cycle ≤ 0): IKEA, Walmart, Costco, airlines with ticket advances.","Profit ≠ liquidity: a profitable firm can still run out of cash.","Repay a short-term loan with cash: WC unchanged; current ratio rises if it was above 1, falls if below 1.","Natural business year-end (low inventories and payables) makes liquidity look better than usual.","Very high liquidity is not automatically good: idle cash earns little."]}]},{"title":"Long-term debt-paying ability","blocks":[{"t":"table","head":["Measure","Formula","Reading"],"rows":[["Times interest earned","EBIT / Interest expense","higher = safer; recurring income only"],["Debt ratio","Total liabilities / Total assets","lower = safer"],["Debt / Equity","Total liabilities / Equity","creditors like low, shareholders like high"],["Debt to tangible net worth","Total liabilities / (Equity − Intangibles)","stricter than D/E"],["Debt quality","CL / Total liabilities","high = mostly short-term, refinancing risk"],["Financial debt / EBITDA","Financial debt / EBITDA","years of EBITDA needed to repay"]]},{"t":"key","title":"Rule","items":["Always take D/E with <b>total liabilities</b>, not only financial debt, unless the question says otherwise. Debt ratio + equity ratio = 100%."]}]},{"title":"Profitability","blocks":[{"t":"table","head":["Measure","Formula"],"rows":[["Gross / operating / net margin","Gross profit / Sales · EBIT / Sales · NI / Sales"],["ROA","EBIT / Total assets (IKEA case: (EBIT + finance income)/TA; operating ROA = EBIT / operating assets)"],["ROA = margin × turnover","EBIT/Sales × Sales/Total assets"],["ROE","NI / Equity"],["DuPont ROE","Sales/Assets × EBIT/Sales × EBT/EBIT × Assets/Equity × NI/EBT"],["Financial leverage effect","(EBT/EBIT) × (Assets/Equity): &gt;1 favourable, =1 neutral, &lt;1 unfavourable"],["ROE before taxes","ROA + (ROA − i) × L/E"],["Asset / fixed-asset turnover","Sales / Total assets · Sales / Fixed assets"]]},{"t":"h3","x":"Reasoning rules"},{"t":"ul","items":["More debt raises ROE only if ROA &gt; cost of debt i. Otherwise ROE falls.","Two levers for ROE: improve ROA (margin or turnover) or raise debt (IKEA case).","Exclude discontinued and extraordinary items when judging recurring profitability.","EBITDA = EBIT + depreciation &amp; amortisation. EBITDAR also adds rents."]}]},{"title":"Market ratios","blocks":[{"t":"table","head":["Measure","Formula"],"rows":[["EPS","Net income / Average common shares"],["Diluted EPS","Net income / (Average shares + dilutive securities)"],["Dividend payout","DPS / Diluted EPS"],["Market-to-book","Market value of equity / Book value of equity"],["P/E","Price / Diluted EPS"]]},{"t":"key","title":"Rule","items":["Diluted EPS &lt; basic EPS, so P/E on diluted EPS is higher (more conservative). High P/E = the market expects strong growth."]}]},{"title":"Case takeaways","blocks":[{"t":"ul","items":["<b>IKEA Ibérica:</b> negative working capital is normal for a retailer with fast-paying customers and slower-paid suppliers. ROE can be improved through ROA or more debt.","<b>IAG (airline):</b> advance ticket sales create unearned revenue, so current liabilities are large. 2020 (COVID) losses turn profitability and market ratios negative.","A negative P/E is not meaningful; the IAG slide shows −2.9 for 2020 while the annex shows 2.9, so quote the sign that your calculation gives and say the ratio is not meaningful."]}]},{"title":"Exam tactics (13 minutes, 5 questions)","blocks":[{"t":"ul","items":["<b>Time:</b> about 2.5 minutes per question. Do the sure ones first; free navigation lets you come back.","<b>Scoring:</b> correct +2, wrong MCQ −0.5, blank 0. A blind guess among 4 options has expected value 0.25×2 − 0.75×0.5 = +0.125, so guess whenever you cannot rule out at least one option. With two options eliminated, always guess (+0.75).","<b>Numeric questions:</b> the penalty is for multiple choice only, so always enter a value.","<b>Calculator:</b> keep formulas written exactly as in the course (EBIT-based ROA, total liabilities in D/E, 365 days).","<b>Check the wording:</b> \"average\" balance, \"gross\" receivables, \"diluted\" EPS, \"before taxes\".","<b>Direction questions:</b> write what happens to numerator and denominator first."]}]}]};
